Interesting: /tmp does not persist between build.sh calls, but /gt/_work/testdir persists (writable root). So the /gt/_work/src is the persistent source; we can create files there and they persist. 

So plan: configure & build libxml2 with ASAN inside /gt/_work/src (in-tree or separate build dir). But careful: the benchmark might rebuild from clean source on each submit? The submit server has its own fixed harness. Our local build is just for experimentation — good.

Note the source in /gt/_work/src is the same as workspace repo-vul/src-vul. Building with ASAN there is fine.

Compile approach: use autotools configure with CC=clang CFLAGS="-fsanitize=address -g -O1" and disable shared libs maybe, then build minimal: we only need libxml2 + a driver. Actually faster: compile just the needed object files? libxml2 is big (~100 .c files) but a full build with make takes maybe 3-6 min. We have time.

Alternative: build with cmake? autotools likely easiest in this container. Configure requires libtool, autoconf etc. Present. Dependencies (zlib, lzma, iconv) optional.

Steps:
1. `autogen.sh` or run configure directly (configure might not be generated yet — no configure script in listing? There was no configure listed. autogen.sh will run autoconf etc.)
2. configure --without-python --without-lzma --without-zlib ... --disable-shared with CC=clang CFLAGS asan.
3. make -j
4. compile a standalone driver main that reads a file, calls LLVMFuzzerInitialize + LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput from fuzz/xml.c with fuzz.c; link with static lib.

Let me first write the driver main locally (workspace), but compile needs fuzz/xml.c etc. in env. We can create /gt/_work/driver.c via build.sh with heredoc. But note: an input file for local test runs: create it via build.sh (as hex/base64) and persist under /gt/_work (e.g. /gt/_work/test1.bin).

But wait, is the ASAN crash going to be affected by environment (libFuzzer not needed)?

Let me also verify that clang version supports ASAN in container. Let's start the build. First autogen+configure. Careful about time — each build.sh command caps at 1800 s; that's enough.

Actually before building, maybe simpler and much faster: The libxml2 source has only 100ish .c files and building with `make -j$(nproc)`... nproc maybe 4-8? Should be OK within ~10 min.

