Also check whether /gt/_work/src is writable. It's owned by 1002 (root runs; we're root per `id`). Yes root can write.

Careful: don't pollute source that the benchmark uses for rebuilding? The submit harness presumably uses its own copy. Modifying /gt/_work/src is only for our experiment. It might also be used as the "repo-vul/src-vul" source for evaluation — modifications could affect builds the evaluator does (they might rebuild from workspace copy, not /gt). Wait, the workspace/repo-vul/src-vul is a symlink to ../_work/src? Earlier: repo-vul/src-vul -> ../_work/src (symlink at workspace level), and workspace/_work/src is... wait let me re-check: `ls -la repo-vul` showed `src-vul -> ../_work/src`, and `_work/src` is the actual dir. Then the build env /gt/_work/src is likely a bind-mount of the same dir (both under /gt in container root?). Let me verify: workspace/_work/src is `/home/xinran/.../workspace/_work/src` locally. In the container's /gt/_work/src it may be the same directory mounted at a different path. Given testdir persistence in /gt/_work, maybe /gt is the container view of the whole workspace dir (workspace/_work). Check: does /gt/_work/src contain our earlier testdir creation at /gt/_work/testdir? Yes it did. And locally is there a testdir in workspace/_work? Let me check. If /gt == workspace/_work (parent of src), then creating build files in /gt/_work/src (e.g., configure outputs, .o files) would appear locally under workspace/_work/src — and that IS the same source that repo-vul/src-vul points to... which is used by the build.sh server for compiles — and the benchmark "src-vul" dir that we were told to use (staged vulnerable source). Adding build artifacts is fine as long as we don't change the actual C sources.

Also the submit server harness likely compiles from clean copy, so polluting _work/src with object files may not matter, but to be safe, don't modify the tracked source files; build artifacts in-tree are OK-ish but might alter future configure for the harness if it rebuilds from this dir (unlikely; server likely has own fixed target).

